      "answer": "The customer whose issue started after the 2026-02-20 taxonomy rollout is BlueHarbor Logistics. Their issue was search relevance degradation in the Event Nexus product, with saved searches returning poor and duplicate results, increasing manual triage time to about 6 hours per day. This was triggered by the taxonomy rollout on 2026-02-20, as reported in the support ticket and confirmed in the renewal negotiation and retro documents [art_8b0063fbb3cb, art_0bccc580184e, art_bd3560dfe194].\n\nThe proof plan proposed to BlueHarbor Logistics to get them comfortable with renewal involved a short-term fix consisting of updating index weighting and adding a translation/mapping layer that maps new taxonomy terms to historical query tokens for the top 20 saved searches. The plan included receiving schema registry export and query logs by 2026-03-19, starting an A/B test in staging on 2026-03-22 lasting 7-10 business days, with validation metrics requiring top-5 correct hit rate >= 80%, no regression in suppression logic, and incident correlation latency under 200ms. If successful, a 1-day production rollout and 72-hour monitoring would follow. A 90-day professional services engagement was offered at $48k fixed price, with a 30% discount if Orchestrator seat expansion was signed concurrently. A conditional credit clause was proposed: if the proof did not meet the agreed accuracy uplift within 10 business days, a one-quarter invoice credit prorated against renewal would apply. This, along with detailed acceptance criteria, risk mitigation including fallback reindexing (estimated 48 hours compute and $25k cost), and a runbook for acceptance testing, was presented in the renewal negotiation and retro documents. The customer agreed to provide necessary data and staging environment access to enable the proof [art_0bccc580184e, art_bd3560dfe194].\n\nNo other customers with issues starting after 2026-02-20 and associated proof plans are documented in the provided evidence.",
